answer:
I can guide you on setting up a process to automate this task using Google Sheets. 

You can create a Google Apps Script that connects to Google Analytics 4 (GA4), extracts the engagement metrics you need, and then updates your Google Sheet with the data. Once set up, this script can run automatically every two weeks without your intervention.

To get started:

1.  Open your Google Sheet and go to Tools > Script editor. This will open the Google Apps Script editor.

2.  Set up a trigger for the script to run automatically every two weeks. In the script editor, click on the "Triggers" icon, then click on the "Create trigger" button. Set the "Run" dropdown to "Function", select the function that will run the script, set the frequency to every 2 weeks, and set the time to run to a suitable time for your organization.

3.  Create a function in the script editor that will fetch the engagement metrics from GA4. Use the Google Analytics Reporting API for this. This will involve setting up an API key and credentials for the script to use. You can use the Google API Console to do this. 

4.  Once the API credentials are set up, you can create a function that uses the Google Analytics Reporting API to fetch the engagement metrics from GA4. The API will return data in a format that you can then process and update your Google Sheet with.

5.  Finally, update the Google Sheet to automatically run the function that fetches the data. This can be done by adding a new trigger that runs the function and then writes the data to the sheet.

Here is a sample code to get you started:

```javascript
function getGA4Data() {
  var apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY';
  var datasetId = 'YOUR_DATASET_ID';
  var metric = 'engagementRate';
  var metricDimension = 'userFlowId';
  var options = {
    'dateTimeMetric': '6 days ago',
    'metrics': [
      {
        'name': metric
      }
    ],
    'dimensions': [
      {
        'name': metricDimension
      }
    ]
  };
  var report = gapi.client.analyticsmanagementreports
   .report({
      'dimensions': options.dimensions,
      'metrics': options.metrics,
      'start_date': options.dateTimeMetric,
      'end_date': options.dateTimeMetric
    });
  report.execute(function(response) {
    var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
    var values = response.rows.map(function(row) {
      return row.values;
    });
    sheet.getRange(sheet.getLastRow() + 1, 1, values.length, values[0].length).setValues(values);
  });
}

function autoRun() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
  getGA4Data();
}

function onOpen() {
  var ui = SpreadsheetApp.getUi();
  // Or DocumentApp or FormApp.
  ui.createMenu('GA4 Integration')
     .addItem('Run Script', 'autoRun')
     .addToUi();
}
```
You will need to fill in your own API key, dataset ID, and metric. This script will fetch the engagement rate from GA4 for the last 6 days and update the Google Sheet with the data. 

However, note that the Google Analytics Reporting API has usage limits, so you may need to check on usage before setting up automated runs. Also, if you make changes to your exec deck that require additional or different metrics, you will need to modify the script accordingly.

I hope this gets you started. Please note that error handling has been left out in this sample code for brevity and simplicity.

